** Set state
The only place `manifolding-keyboard-state' is assigned, so the model line
accommodation lives here rather than in each of the 15 state units. Emacs
does not recompute a mode line because a buffer-local variable changed, and
the bar shows the *name* of the state, so a state change has to be announced.
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defun manifolding-keyboard-set-state (state)
  "Set current manifolding-keyboard STATE."
  ;; Normalise the input to a symbol. The state machine is keyed by symbols
  ;; and calls `manifolding-keyboard-get-state-mode' on the result, but it is
  ;; also driven from `change-major-mode-after-body-hook', and under polymode
  ;; the mode name arriving here is a *string* — poly-org-mode yields "org",
  ;; not a symbol. Left unconverted that is a wrong-type-argument inside the
  ;; state table lookup, thrown from the middle of a mode-line compose.
  (when (stringp state)
    (setq state (intern state)))
  (let ((previous manifolding-keyboard-state))
    (when (and manifolding-keyboard-state (not (equal manifolding-keyboard-state state)))
      (funcall (manifolding-keyboard-get-state-mode manifolding-keyboard-state) -1))
    (when state
      (funcall (manifolding-keyboard-get-state-mode state) 1))
    (setq manifolding-keyboard-state state)
    (unless (equal previous manifolding-keyboard-state)
      ;; Model line accommodation. Guarded on fboundp so the keyboard has no
      ;; dependency on the modeline module — either can be absent.
      (when (fboundp 'manifolding-modeline--state-changed)
        (manifolding-modeline--state-changed)))))
#+end_src
** Model line accommodation
The bar reads the state from `manifolding-keyboard-state' and shows it in
full — `normal`, `insert`, `presentation`, `git-timemachine` — not as a
single letter. Fifteen two-letter codes are unreadable at a glance and, on a
narrow bar, no narrower than the names are; the width is not the constraint,
legibility is.

This is the one function the model line consumes, so there is a single
definition of "what the state reads as" and no second copy to drift.
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defun manifolding-keyboard-modeline-state-name ()
  "Full name of the current state, for the mode line.
Reads `manifolding-keyboard-state' directly — the bar never keeps its own
copy, so the two can never disagree. Returns nil before the keyboard has
assigned a state, and the segment treats nil as \"show nothing\".

Total over the value's type, not just symbols. The state is normally a
symbol, but the keyboard is also driven from `change-major-mode-after-body-
hook', and under polymode the mode name reaching `set-state' is a *string*
— `poly-org-mode` yields \"org\". Calling `symbol-name' on that is a
wrong-type-argument, and it fires from inside the mode-line compose, so it
takes the whole bar down rather than just this segment."
  (let ((state manifolding-keyboard-state))
    (cond ((null state) nil)
          ((symbolp state) (symbol-name state))
          ((stringp state) state)
          (t (format "%s" state)))))
#+end_src
